"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/puerto-cabello.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Puerto Cabello</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Puerto Cabello is a port city on the Caribbean coast of Venezuela, in Carabobo state, with one of the best natural harbours in the country.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cortes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/puerto-cortes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Puerto Cortés</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Puerto Cortés is a city on the Caribbean coast of north-western Honduras and the country's main seaport.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/puerto-limon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/puerto-limon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Puerto Limón</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Puerto Limón, often called simply Limón, is the main Caribbean port of Costa Rica and capital of Limón Province.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rican-nationalist-party"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/puerto-rican-nationalist-party.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Puerto Rican Nationalist Party</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Puerto Rican Nationalist Party was founded in 1922 to seek independence from the United States and, under Pedro Albizu Campos from 1930, turned to militant action.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/puerto-rico.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Puerto Rico</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Puerto Rico is an island in the Caribbean, colonised by Spain from the early sixteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pugalur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pugalur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pugalur</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pugalur is a town in Karur district of Tamil Nadu, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/puhar"><span class="enc-name">Puhar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Puhar, also called Kaveripattinam, was the main port of the early Chola kings at the mouth of the Kaveri River in Tamil Nadu, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/puig-des-molins"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/puig-des-molins.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Puig des Molins</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Puig des Molins is an ancient necropolis on a hill in the city of Ibiza, used from the Phoenician period in the 7th century BCE through Punic and Roman times.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pujavaliya"><span class="enc-name">Pujavaliya</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pujavaliya is a classic of Sinhala prose written in the 13th century by the monk Mayurapada Buddhaputra during the Dambadeniya period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pula"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pula.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pula</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pula is a city at the southern tip of the Istrian peninsula in Croatia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pulaar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pulaar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pulaar</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Pulaar is the variety of the Fula language spoken in the Senegal River valley, the old region of Futa Toro, and in Senegal, Mauritania, the Gambia and Guinea-Bissau.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-i"><span class="enc-name">Pulakeshin I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pulakeshin I was the founder of the Chalukya dynasty of Badami in Karnataka, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pulakeshin-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pulakeshin II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pulakeshin II was the greatest king of the Chalukyas of Badami, in the western Deccan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pulses"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pulses.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pulses</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Pulses are the edible seeds of leguminous plants, such as lentils, peas, chickpeas and beans.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pulvar-river"><span class="enc-name">Pulvar River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pulvar is a river in Fars Province, south-western Iran, which flows past Pasargadae and Persepolis to join the Kor River on the plain of Marvdasht.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pumiyaton"><span class="enc-name">Pumiyaton</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pumiyaton was the last Phoenician king of Kition in Cyprus, reigning from about 362 to 312 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/punakha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/punakha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Punakha</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Punakha is a town in western Bhutan where the Pho Chhu and Mo Chhu rivers meet.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/punctation-of-olmutz"><span class="enc-name">Punctation of Olmütz</span><span class="enc-type">Treaty · Crisis · 1850</span><span class="enc-snip">The Punctation of Olmütz was an agreement signed on 29 November 1850 at Olmütz in Moravia between Prussia and Austria, with Russian backing for Austria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pune"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pune.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pune</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pune, formerly Poona, is a city on the Deccan plateau in Maharashtra, western India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pungnap-toseong"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pungnap-toseong.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pungnap Toseong</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pungnap Toseong is a large earthen-walled site on the south bank of the Han River in Songpa, south-eastern Seoul, built around the third century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pungo-andongo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pungo-andongo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pungo Andongo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pungo Andongo is a massif of giant black rock formations in Malanje Province, Angola, which served as a royal stronghold of the kingdom of Ndongo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire"><span class="enc-name">Punic Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Punic Empire, or Carthaginian Empire, was a maritime state centred on Carthage in present-day Tunisia, founded as a colony of Tyre and traditionally dated to 814 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/punic-wars.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Punic Wars</span><span class="enc-type">War · 264–146 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Punic Wars were three wars fought between Rome and Carthage between 264 and 146 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/punisa-racic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/punisa-racic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Puniša Račić</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Puniša Račić was a Montenegrin Serb politician of the People's Radical Party.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/punjab"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/punjab.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Punjab</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Punjab is a region of the north-western Indian subcontinent, divided today between Pakistan and India, named after its five rivers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/punjab-states-agency"><span class="enc-name">Punjab States Agency</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Punjab States Agency was a political agency of British India, created in 1921 to manage relations between the British government and the princely states of the…</span></a></li></ul>
